Henry I Captured His Own Brother Henry I made his knights fight on foot, then sprang a concealed fourth division against the enemy flank, taking his brother Robert prisoner and ending the fight for Normandy. The British History Podcast British History Podcast 505 – The Battle of Tinchebray Medieval history Normandy England War decisions Source criticism Orderic Vitalis Henry of Huntingdon siege castle\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-britishhist-亨利一世在坦什布赖靠下马步战加伏兵生擒长兄/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.thebritishhistorypodcast.com/logo.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">48:01</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"hist\"><span class=\"src\">The British History Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Dismounted Knights and a Hidden Ambush: How Henry I Captured His Own Brother</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Henry I made his knights fight on foot, then sprang a concealed fourth division against the enemy flank, taking his brother Robert prisoner and ending the fight for Normandy.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Medieval history</span><span class=\"tag\">Normandy</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"DNA Repair Is the Key to Long Life: A Bowhead Whale Repair Protein Is Already in Human Trials New work finds that nearly half of hospitalized COVID-19 patients had dormant viruses reactivate in their bodies, with higher inflammation and cell damage a year later; meanwhile what long-lived animals like the bowhead whale share is not tumor-suppressor genes but stronger DNA repair, and drugs built on that idea have reached human trials. Nature Podcast Nature Podcast Briefing Chat: Is DNA repair the secret to a long life? Whales and mole-rats offer tantalizing hints Long COVID Dormant viruses DNA repair Anti-aging Human trials Bowhead whales dormant virus double-strand break CIRBP (cold-inducible RNA-binding protein) Sirt6 Fucoidan NAD+ (nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-naturepod-长新冠或源于休眠病毒-dna修复或为长寿关键/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://assets.pippa.io/shows/61b9f3b71a8cbe675f3cedcb/1786105817525-2bb986fa-0e50-42e5-a5cd-1ab1f3bc9517.jpeg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"N\"><span class=\"dur\">13:52</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Nature Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>DNA Repair Is the Key to Long Life: A Bowhead Whale Repair Protein Is Already in Human Trials</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">New work finds that nearly half of hospitalized COVID-19 patients had dormant viruses reactivate in their bodies, with higher inflammation and cell damage a year later; meanwhile what long-lived animals like the bowhead whale share is not tumor-suppressor genes but stronger DNA repair, and drugs built on that idea have reached human trials.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Long COVID</span><span class=\"tag\">Dormant viruses</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"Don&#x27;t Teach Your Kid Empathy; First Let Them See You Listening Getting kids to disagree well is not a matter of teaching empathy or perspective-taking — it is a matter of proving with words that you are listening; the HEAR framework and the curiosity sandwich can be used immediately. Raising Good Humans Raising Good Humans How to Disagree With Your Kids Without Fighting Parent-child talk Disagreement Listening Harvard research Conversation frameworks Parenting HEAR framework curiosity sandwich false polarization hedging\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-raisinggh-别只让孩子换位思考-要让他看见你在听/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/64875c2a-9023-11f1-b6bf-e7aff692fa61/image/dca88085db0b2fe33b742b29ede07799.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"R\"><span class=\"dur\">55:22</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Raising Good Humans</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Don&#x27;t Teach Your Kid Empathy; First Let Them See You Listening</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Getting kids to disagree well is not a matter of teaching empathy or perspective-taking — it is a matter of proving with words that you are listening; the HEAR framework and the curiosity sandwich can be used immediately.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Parent-child talk</span><span class=\"tag\">Disagreement</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"Screening embryos isn&#x27;t doing everything possible — it&#x27;s moral blackmail Genetic screening can lower disease risk, but the statistics are routinely oversold, and the practice may deepen social inequality. Parents should aim for &quot;good enough&quot; rather than optimal, and stay wary of the moral blackmail commercial firms trade on. The Gray Area with Sean Illing The Gray Area What does a parent owe their child? Genetic screening Reproductive ethics IVF Polygenic testing Value pluralism PGT-A polygenic testing pleiotropy satisficing\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-grayarea-胚胎筛选-父母该为孩子做到哪一步/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/a4ca29cc-381a-11f1-a32d-c7b32dc2aeff/image/09d3576515488959f432d219d5c7416b.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">27:47</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">The Gray Area with Sean Illing</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>Screening embryos isn&#x27;t doing everything possible — it&#x27;s moral blackmail</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Genetic screening can lower disease risk, but the statistics are routinely oversold, and the practice may deepen social inequality. Parents should aim for &quot;good enough&quot; rather than optimal, and stay wary of the moral blackmail commercial firms trade on.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Genetic screening</span><span class=\"tag\">Reproductive ethics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"An Actress&#x27;s Contract Locked In Her Body: Gain Weight and You Breach It, Lose Weight and It Kills You In the 1920s the actress Molly was shelved by her studio for gaining weight and ended up on an operating table; in the 1990s a bride named Mary died from the diet drug fen-phen. Once a body becomes a commercial asset, the punishment and the shortcut lead to the same place. Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford Cautionary Tales The Weight of Fame: A Hollywood Tragedy Hollywood Body standards Diet drugs Business ethics Contract terms Medical risk lipectomy fen-phen primary pulmonary hypertension (PPH)\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-07-cautionary-制片厂的合同里写着-女演员的身体不得改变/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/playlist/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/c0ae8c6e-22f0-4e9b-ac1c-ae390037ac53/7f5a4714-6b10-4ccf-a424-ae390037ac70/image.jpg?t=1650471153&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">41:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Cautionary Tales with Tim Harford</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0807</span></div>\n<h2>An Actress&#x27;s Contract Locked In Her Body: Gain Weight and You Breach It, Lose Weight and It Kills You</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In the 1920s the actress Molly was shelved by her studio for gaining weight and ended up on an operating table; in the 1990s a bride named Mary died from the diet drug fen-phen. Once a body becomes a commercial asset, the punishment and the shortcut lead to the same place.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Hollywood</span><span class=\"tag\">Body standards</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AMD Buys Talus: Burning AI Models Into Silicon, 17,000 Tokens a Second AMD has acquired Talus, a startup that builds custom chips for one specific AI model each and reaches 17,000 tokens per second. It marks a turn in AI inference toward non-programmable, model-specific silicon. TechTechPotato TechTechPotato Why did AMD just buy this REALLY WEIRD chip company? AI chips AMD Talus Model customization Inference acceleration SRAM reticle size agentic AI\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-techtechpotato-amd收购talus-押注模型定制芯片的未来/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://i4.ytimg.com/vi/3MKRjt59hh4/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"T\"><span class=\"dur\">16:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">TechTechPotato</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>AMD Buys Talus: Burning AI Models Into Silicon, 17,000 Tokens a Second</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">AMD has acquired Talus, a startup that builds custom chips for one specific AI model each and reaches 17,000 tokens per second. It marks a turn in AI inference toward non-programmable, model-specific silicon.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>5</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI chips</span><span class=\"tag\">AMD</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"cn\"\n data-hay=\"AI Didn&#x27;t Kill Jobs — Agent Teamwork Is the Real Bottleneck Yangqing Jia (贾扬清) traces fifteen years of AI from Caffe through two startups: model capability is already good enough, and the bottleneck is getting a group of agents to collaborate the way a human team does. He also argues flatly that AI has not caused mass layoffs — it is just the scapegoat. What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道 科技早知道 贾扬清：我所经历的「人工智能已死」到「AI 颠覆世界」的数年巨变丨串台「声东击西」S10E24 AI Startups Open source Agents Silicon Valley LLMs Caffe AlexNet Agent FAIR\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-whatsnext-单个智能体再强也成不了团队-ai需要生产关系/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://media24.fireside.fm/file/fireside-images-2024/podcasts/images/4/4931937e-0184-4c61-a658-6b03c254754d/cover.jpg?v=10\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"W\"><span class=\"dur\">2:10:08</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"cn\"><span class=\"src\" lang=\"zh\">What&#x27;s Next｜科技早知道</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>AI Didn&#x27;t Kill Jobs — Agent Teamwork Is the Real Bottleneck</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Yangqing Jia (<span lang=\"zh\">贾扬清</span>) traces fifteen years of AI from Caffe through two startups: model capability is already good enough, and the bottleneck is getting a group of agents to collaborate the way a human team does. He also argues flatly that AI has not caused mass layoffs — it is just the scapegoat.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI</span><span class=\"tag\">Startups</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"sci\"\n data-hay=\"Yo-yo dieting doesn&#x27;t wreck your metabolism; habits, not weight, decide lifespan Weight cycling — yo-yo dieting — does not permanently break your metabolism, and in ordinary people the weight that comes back rarely costs you muscle. What actually shapes health and lifespan is the healthy habits themselves, not the swings in weight. Science Vs Science Vs Weight Loss: Why Won&#x27;t It Stick? Weight cycling Weight loss Metabolism Inflammation Healthy habits Randomized trials weight cycling visceral fat macrophage\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-sciencevs-体重循环不毁代谢-健康行为比体重重要/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/f521b120-2c30-11e6-b5f3-63ee984ee1a4/image/e7470287efbfa0aff068175bb6c31805.jpg?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"S\"><span class=\"dur\">39:39</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"sci\"><span class=\"src\">Science Vs</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>Yo-yo dieting doesn&#x27;t wreck your metabolism; habits, not weight, decide lifespan</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Weight cycling — yo-yo dieting — does not permanently break your metabolism, and in ordinary people the weight that comes back rarely costs you muscle. What actually shapes health and lifespan is the healthy habits themselves, not the swings in weight.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Weight cycling</span><span class=\"tag\">Weight loss</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"The US joins Japan to intervene on the yen: selling euros, not dollars, and betting the BOJ hikes In a rare move, the US Treasury has intervened on the yen alongside Japan, selling euros rather than dollars and putting the FIMA repo facility to use. Brad Setser argues the intervention will work as long as the Bank of Japan raises rates, and that Japan&#x27;s fiscal position is far better than America&#x27;s. Odd Lots Odd Lots Brad Setser on the US&#x27;s Unusual Japanese Yen Intervention Yen FX intervention Bank of Japan Treasuries Fiscal policy FIMA Repo Facility ESF (Exchange Stabilization Fund) Primary Balance Carry Trade Big Mac Index\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-oddlots-美国为何联手日本干预日元-新工具与旧逻辑/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://www.omnycontent.com/d/playlist/e73c998e-6e60-432f-8610-ae210140c5b1/8a94442e-5a74-4fa2-8b8d-ae27003a8d6b/982f5071-765c-403d-969d-ae27003a8d83/image.jpg?t=1681322812&amp;size=Medium\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"O\"><span class=\"dur\">42:00</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Odd Lots</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>The US joins Japan to intervene on the yen: selling euros, not dollars, and betting the BOJ hikes</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">In a rare move, the US Treasury has intervened on the yen alongside Japan, selling euros rather than dollars and putting the FIMA repo facility to use. Brad Setser argues the intervention will work as long as the Bank of Japan raises rates, and that Japan&#x27;s fiscal position is far better than America&#x27;s.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>2</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Yen</span><span class=\"tag\">FX intervention</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"222-nanometre lamps kill pathogens and still sell a few hundred a year; the bottleneck isn&#x27;t the technology Far UVC uses 222-nanometre light to inactivate pathogens in the air while leaving the outer layer of human tissue unharmed; a South African TB trial is already seeing 90% suppression, yet the whole world buys only a few hundred units a year. The real bottleneck is how far the knowledge has spread. Complex Systems Complex Systems The economics of putting germicidal light in every room, with Misha Gurevich and Vivian Belenky of Aerolamp far UVC airborne disease public health startups building codes cost curves far UVC krypton chloride excimer lamp ASHRAE 241 R0 hygiene hypothesis\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-06-complexsys-222纳米杀菌灯便宜有效-缺的是认知而非技术/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/_SmyPmSli4we-X2h7KiJ-wvjf2umakfC-X_hqszS_rE/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS8xMTZi/ODRmODgzZTZiNjAz/N2YzNmQxY2RkY2M4/YTE0Ni5wbmc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">1:16:20</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Complex Systems</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0806</span></div>\n<h2>222-nanometre lamps kill pathogens and still sell a few hundred a year; the bottleneck isn&#x27;t the technology</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Far UVC uses 222-nanometre light to inactivate pathogens in the air while leaving the outer layer of human tissue unharmed; a South African TB trial is already seeing 90% suppression, yet the whole world buys only a few hundred units a year. The real bottleneck is how far the knowledge has spread.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">far UVC</span><span class=\"tag\">airborne disease</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ideas\"\n data-hay=\"The Soviet Union forged its women into supermen and spoiled its men into uselessness 27 million Soviet citizens died in the Second World War, and men became a scarce resource; the state used an abortion ban, rewards for children fathered outside marriage, and the decriminalization of domestic violence to forge its women into supermen and spoil its men into uselessness — an order that still underwrites Putin&#x27;s patriarchal mobilization. Conversations with Tyler Conversations with Tyler Julia Ioffe on Russian Women, Missing Men, and the Roots of Autocracy Russian society Gender politics Population policy Feminist history Putin politics woman question single sex family gender essentialism pronatalist policy steam valve\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-05-tyler-苏联把女人炼成超人-把男人宠成废物/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://static.libsyn.com/p/assets/9/3/b/c/93bcc9e054e0ad4fd959afa2a1bf1c87/JuliaIoffe-libsyn-20260804-lpkt75rsui.png\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"C\"><span class=\"dur\">55:14</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ideas\"><span class=\"src\">Conversations with Tyler</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0805</span></div>\n<h2>The Soviet Union forged its women into supermen and spoiled its men into uselessness</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">27 million Soviet citizens died in the Second World War, and men became a scarce resource; the state used an abortion ban, rewards for children fathered outside marriage, and the decriminalization of domestic violence to forge its women into supermen and spoil its men into uselessness — an order that still underwrites Putin&#x27;s patriarchal mobilization.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Russian society</span><span class=\"tag\">Gender politics</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"biz\"\n data-hay=\"Big Tech&#x27;s off-balance-sheet debt isn&#x27;t fraud — nobody reads the footnotes The supposed $1.65 trillion of hidden debt is not Enron-style fraud but long-term leases on data centres that aren&#x27;t running yet, plus purchase commitments — legal, but buried in the footnotes. The real danger is that the AI giants are financing each other and telling their story through adjusted earnings. Patrick Boyle Patrick Boyle Big Tech&#x27;s Hidden Debt Problem AI capex Off-balance-sheet debt Accounting gloss Footnote disclosure Valuation bubble off-balance-sheet vehicle vendor financing big market delusion incomplete revelation hypothesis limits of arbitrage\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-patrickboyle-表外债务不是欺诈-真正的风险是没人读脚注/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://d3t3ozftmdmh3i.cloudfront.net/staging/podcast_uploaded_nologo/42184536/48d3d010465c2cda.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"P\"><span class=\"dur\">32:18</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"biz\"><span class=\"src\">Patrick Boyle</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>Big Tech&#x27;s off-balance-sheet debt isn&#x27;t fraud — nobody reads the footnotes</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The supposed $1.65 trillion of hidden debt is not Enron-style fraud but long-term leases on data centres that aren&#x27;t running yet, plus purchase commitments — legal, but buried in the footnotes. The real danger is that the AI giants are financing each other and telling their story through adjusted earnings.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AI capex</span><span class=\"tag\">Off-balance-sheet debt</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"AI revenue up 700%, but on grunt work AI still trails humans 6x The 2026 evidence on AI progress: revenue exploding, coding agents maturing, grunt-work tasks still lagging; timelines have shortened by about a year, but the long run is still open. 80,000 Hours Podcast 80,000 Hours What the hell happened with AGI timelines in 2026? – Rob Wiblin AGI AI agents Revenue growth Grunt work Inference scaling Timelines RLVR METR ECI GDPval Vending-Bench 2 AI Village\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-eightythousand-agi-时间线提前一年-但脏活仍是瓶颈/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://img.transistorcdn.com/49BMpcjJuN6K9_2fdbqihTyXQf3o0FlvZyUMgVi1mcA/rs:fill:0:0:1/w:1400/h:1400/q:60/mb:500000/aHR0cHM6Ly9pbWct/dXBsb2FkLXByb2R1/Y3Rpb24udHJhbnNp/c3Rvci5mbS9lNWM3/NWRiNmRjMTM0MzRm/MzlmOGYyMjkxMmE5/YmM0YS5qcGc.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"8\"><span class=\"dur\">49:28</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">80,000 Hours Podcast</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>AI revenue up 700%, but on grunt work AI still trails humans 6x</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">The 2026 evidence on AI progress: revenue exploding, coding agents maturing, grunt-work tasks still lagging; timelines have shortened by about a year, but the long run is still open.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">AGI</span><span class=\"tag\">AI agents</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"parent\"\n data-hay=\"Getting cut from the friend group is not a verdict — it is chemical bonding Being frozen out by a friend group, or needing to end a friendship yourself, are both normal chapters of adolescence; the most useful thing a parent can do is hand the kid perspective and specific lines to say, not charge in on their behalf. Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens Ask Lisa 283: ENCORE: Best of Ask Lisa: Friendship Breakups &amp; Conflict Adolescence Friendship Bullying Social conflict Parent-child communication relational aggression queen bee blame the parent two-faced\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-asklisa-友谊破裂是成长章节-不是人生判决/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://megaphone.imgix.net/podcasts/c55e4104-cbbe-11ec-93d2-ab245e93324f/image/478fcfa676f260bc9a5628875ab7e0af.png?ixlib=rails-4.3.1&amp;max-w=3000&amp;max-h=3000&amp;fit=crop&amp;auto=format,compress&amp;w=400&amp;auto=format\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"A\"><span class=\"dur\">23:42</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"parent\"><span class=\"src\">Ask Lisa: The Psychology of Raising Tweens &amp; Teens</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>Getting cut from the friend group is not a verdict — it is chemical bonding</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Being frozen out by a friend group, or needing to end a friendship yourself, are both normal chapters of adolescence; the most useful thing a parent can do is hand the kid perspective and specific lines to say, not charge in on their behalf.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>8</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Adolescence</span><span class=\"tag\">Friendship</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"A $250,000 Salary Wasn&#x27;t Enough, So He Extorted His Own Company Ubiquiti&#x27;s cloud team lead decided $250,000 a year was not enough, staged an intrusion into his own company and demanded 25 bitcoin — and was pinned down by a MAC address logged by his own Ubiquiti router. Six years in prison. Darknet Diaries Darknet Diaries 178: Ubiquiti Insider Attack Corporate Extortion GitHub VPN Cloud Security Forensics least privilege VPN kill switch MAC address\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-04-darknetdiaries-ubiquiti-内鬼自导自演勒索比特币-败在自家路由器/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://f.prxu.org/7057/e5c784a5-d775-435a-bbb4-cdb885fbf87a/images/70961159-7e87-45f8-9815-750384854b41/Ep178_Square.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"D\"><span class=\"dur\">36:12</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Darknet Diaries</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0804</span></div>\n<h2>A $250,000 Salary Wasn&#x27;t Enough, So He Extorted His Own Company</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Ubiquiti&#x27;s cloud team lead decided $250,000 a year was not enough, staged an intrusion into his own company and demanded 25 bitcoin — and was pinned down by a MAC address logged by his own Ubiquiti router. Six years in prison.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>7</b> Points</span>\n<span class=\"badge\"><b>6</b> Quotes</span>\n<span class=\"tag\">Insider Attack</span><span class=\"tag\">Corporate Extortion</span>\n</div></div></a>", "<a class=\"card\" data-card data-cat=\"ai\"\n data-hay=\"Quantizing more layers can be more faithful: the errors cancel out Once you have picked the right layers, quantizing more of them actually preserves more fidelity — the errors cancel each other out. By the same logic, a 10x speedup is not one breakthrough: it is quantization, the speculator and PD disaggregation each roughly doubling and stacking up. Latent Space Latent Space The Inference Engineering Masterclass — Philip Kiely &amp; Ali Taha, Baseten Inference optimization Quantization Speculative decoding GPU hardware Video generation Open models speculative decoding (spec dec) PD disaggregation (prefill/decode separation) NVFP4 mega kernel expert parallelism KV cache compaction\" href=\"/podcast/en/p/2026-08-03-latentspace-量化更多层反而更准-因为误差会互相抵消/\">\n<div class=\"cover\"><img src=\"https://substackcdn.com/feed/podcast/1084089/post/209198968/3c0bb877670d79fe2147a4eec93b16d6.jpg\" alt=\"\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" data-initial=\"L\"><span class=\"dur\">1:41:29</span></div>\n<div class=\"card-body\">\n<div class=\"kicker\" data-cat=\"ai\"><span class=\"src\">Latent Space</span>\n<span class=\"date\">0803</span></div>\n<h2>Quantizing more layers can be more faithful: the errors cancel out</h2>\n<p class=\"dek\">Once you have picked the right layers, quantizing more of them actually preserves more fidelity — the errors cancel each other out. By the same logic, a 10x speedup is not one breakthrough: it is quantization, the speculator and PD disaggregation each roughly doubling and stacking up.</p>\n<div class=\"card-foot\">\n<span class=\"badge\